 ## Overview
-This method extracts a topologically correct isosurface from a volumetric scalar field by computing exact intersection points within each voxel using ray intersection with a trilinear interpolation model [S1, S2][S3]. It solves the topological ambiguities inherent in standard Marching Cubes (MC) and avoids the requirement for gradient/Hermite data needed by standard Dual Contouring [S1][S3]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a pre-computed look-up table of cell diagonals to determine the minimum number of rays required to capture all disjoint surface components and tunnels within a cell [S1].
+This method extracts a topologically correct isosurface from a volumetric scalar field by computing exact intersection points within each voxel using ray intersection with a trilinear interpolation model [S1, S2][S3]. It solves the topological ambiguities inherent in standard Marching Cubes (MC) and avoids the requirement for gradient or Hermite data needed by standard Dual Contouring [S1][S3]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a pre-computed look-up table of cell diagonals to determine the minimum number of rays required to capture all disjoint surface components and tunnels within a cell [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 ### Inputs and Outputs
 **Inputs:**
-*   A structured rectilinear hexahedral grid with scalar values $T_{ijk}$ at vertices $(i,j,k) \in \{0,1\}^3$.
-*   An isovalue $I$.
